---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const premCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'claim probability', min: 0.01, max: 0.2, step: 0.005, value: 0.05, unit: '' },
    { name: 'severity', label: 'average claim size', min: 1000, max: 100000, step: 1000, value: 20000, unit: '$' },
    { name: 'policies', label: 'number of policies', min: 100, max: 10000, step: 100, value: 2000, unit: '' },
    { name: 'load', label: 'profit loading', min: 0, max: 0.3, step: 0.01, value: 0.1, unit: '' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'expected claims', expr: 'p*severity*policies', unit: '$' },
    { label: 'premium per policy (+load)', expr: 'p*severity*(1+load)', unit: '$' },
    { label: 'std dev of total claims', expr: 'sqrt(policies*p*(1-p))*severity', unit: '$' },
  ],
};

const probCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Uncertainty has structure', note: 'Single events are unpredictable; ensembles are not. The insurer cannot know who crashes — only how many, on average.' },
    { math: '2 · Model frequency and severity', note: 'Two random pieces: how many claims (frequency) and how large each is (severity). Total cost is their combination.' },
    { math: '3 · Expectation sets the price floor', note: 'E[total] = n·p·E[claim]. Price below expected cost and ruin is arithmetic, not bad luck.' },
    { math: '4 · Variance sets the capital', note: 'Even a fair price fluctuates. The spread of outcomes (std dev) tells the insurer how much capital absorbs a bad year.' },
    { math: '5 · The law of large numbers is the business', note: 'Pooling more policies shrinks relative variance — scale itself is the oldest risk-reduction technology.' },
  ],
};

const atJobsMatch = {
  "prompt": "Two numbers run the entire business — match each concept to its job:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "expectation",
      "right": "the long-run average — sets the floor for the price"
    },
    {
      "left": "variance",
      "right": "how far outcomes can wander — sizes the capital for bad years"
    },
    {
      "left": "probability",
      "right": "a number between 0 and 1 measuring how likely an event is"
    },
    {
      "left": "pooling more policies",
      "right": "shrinks the relative uncertainty — scale is the oldest risk-reduction technology"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Every other idea in this lesson is elaboration on that pair: expectation prices, variance capitalises."
};

const atBayesSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this fraud-screening answer is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "The model flags 99% of fraudulent claims and 5% of honest ones; 1% of all claims are fraudulent.",
    "Out of 10,000 claims, 100 are fraudulent, and 99 of those get flagged.",
    "5% of the 9,900 honest claims also get flagged — 495 false alarms.",
    "Among the 594 flags, 99 are real fraud: 99 ÷ 594 ≈ 16.7 percent.",
    "So a flagged claim is 99 percent likely to be fraudulent — the model catches 99% of fraud."
  ],
  "errorLine": 4,
  "correction": "A flagged claim is fraudulent only about 16.7% of the time — honest claims vastly outnumber fraudulent ones.",
  "why": "The 99% is the true-positive rate, not the probability the flag is right. Base rates rule: nearly five out of six flags are false alarms."
};

const atIndependenceBuckets = {
  "prompt": "Independence is the licence to multiply. File each pairing:",
  "buckets": [
    "Plausibly independent",
    "Dependent"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "car accidents by separate drivers in separate towns",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "fire losses across the flats of one building",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "policies sharing one flood zone",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "two distant drivers' accidents on the same icy morning",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "2,000 similar policies each facing their own small fire chance",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "The test is the multiplication rule, never the vibe: learning one event must not change the probability of the other. One fire takes the whole floor; one icy morning takes both drivers."
};

const atDistCloze = {
  "text": "The %%0%% counts successes in a fixed number of independent trials; the %%1%% counts rare events arriving at a steady rate; the %%2%% models waiting times and is memoryless; the %%3%% emerges when many small independent effects add up.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "binomial"
    ],
    [
      "Poisson",
      "poisson"
    ],
    [
      "exponential"
    ],
    [
      "normal",
      "normal distribution",
      "bell curve"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "One name per blank — counts, arrivals, waits, sums."
};

const atBayesJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "P(fraud given flag) equals P(flag given fraud).",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "17 percent vs 99 percent — the same evidence, two very different probabilities. Confusing them is the prosecutor's fallacy."
    },
    {
      "s": "The prosecutor's fallacy ignores how many other people also match.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "'One-in-a-million match' says nothing about guilt while a million-odd people also match."
    },
    {
      "s": "A 99-percent-accurate test means a flagged claim is 99 percent likely to be fraudulent.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Base rates rule the answer — here it is about 16.7 percent."
    },
    {
      "s": "Bayes' theorem converts 'probability of the evidence if the cause is true' into 'probability of the cause given the evidence'.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "That reversal is the whole trick — and it sounds like a formality until it earns its keep."
    }
  ]
};

const atSdEstimate = {
  "prompt": "The village at scale: 2,000,000 dollars of expected claims, standard deviation about 195,000. Roughly what per cent of the expected total is that typical miss?",
  "unit": "%",
  "answer": 10,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "195,000 ÷ 2,000,000 ≈ 10% — and quadrupling the book only doubles the standard deviation (√n), so the relative uncertainty halves.",
  "context": "That 1-over-√n shrinkage is the law of large numbers wearing a business suit."
};

const atMeanPredict = {
  "q": "A press release quotes the 'average claim', and the number is smaller than the expected loss in the rate filing. Why?",
  "choices": [
    "Because the press release rounds down",
    "Because heavy tails put the mean above the median — the typical claim is small, but rare enormous claims drag the mean up",
    "Because the press release counts only claims that were actually paid"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "Most claims are fender-benders; the rare totals-a-factory claim really arrives and really must be paid. Price the mean, capitalise the tail."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/actuarial-science/foundational/probability-for-actuaries">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Actuarial Science — the subject behind insurance,
    pensions and the pricing of risk. You need no prior knowledge at all. No statistics, no
    probability, no calculus. Just percentages, averages, and one square root, each used
    slowly and explained when it appears. Every new word is defined the moment it shows up.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each concept with its job in the business" widget="match" config={atJobsMatch} />
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why one policy is unpredictable but a pool of thousands is not;</li>
    <li>use conditional probability and Bayes' theorem to turn new evidence into an updated chance — and spot the classic trap in "the test is 99 percent accurate";</li>
    <li>match the standard distributions — binomial, Poisson, exponential, normal, heavy-tailed — to the situations they model;</li>
    <li>compute an expected loss and a standard deviation, and say what each number is for;</li>
    <li>explain how growing the pool shrinks the relative uncertainty, and why that is the whole business model.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph carries one idea. If a formula appears, walk it step by
    step — nothing here is meant to be skimmed.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a village that insures itself</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a village of 100 houses. Each year, each house has a small chance of burning
    down. For any one family, a fire would be ruinous — so the villagers make a deal. Every
    family pays a little money into a common fund at the start of the year, and any family
    whose house burns receives enough from the fund to rebuild. Nobody can say <em>whose</em>
    house will burn. Yet the village treasurer can say, confidently, roughly <em>how many</em>
    fires the village should expect — and therefore how much each family must pay in.
  </p>
  <p>
    That leap is the whole subject. A single event is unpredictable. A large group of
    similar events is remarkably predictable. <strong>Probability</strong> is the
    mathematics of that predictability: it assigns events a number between 0 (impossible)
    and 1 (certain) that says how likely they are. <strong>Actuarial science</strong> is
    probability applied to money — setting prices and reserves for risks like fires,
    accidents and deaths.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two numbers run the entire business. The <strong>expectation</strong> of the losses is
    the long-run average — it sets the floor for the price. The <strong>variance</strong>
    measures how far the outcome can wander from that average — it sets the capital that
    absorbs the bad years. Every other idea in this lesson is elaboration on that pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one house is a gamble; a hundred houses are a forecast.
    Probability measures the uncertainty, expectation sets the price floor, and variance
    sets the capital. The five steps below tell the same story from uncertainty to a
    defensible price — click through them now.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from uncertainty to a defensible price" widget="equationStep" config={probCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the five steps and watch the same two
    questions recur at every one — what price to charge, and what capital to hold. Steps 3
    and 4 assign the two jobs: expectation sets the floor for the price, variance sizes the
    cushion that absorbs a bad year. Step 5 is the punchline that makes the business
    possible — pooling more policies shrinks the relative uncertainty, so scale itself is
    the oldest risk-reduction technology.
  </p>

  <h2>General probability and combinatorics</h2>
  <p>
    Probabilities change when information arrives — and the tool for that change is
    <em>conditional probability</em>: the probability of an event, given that something
    else is known to be true. Its inversion formula, <em>Bayes' theorem</em>, converts
    "the probability of the evidence if the cause is true" into "the probability of the
    cause given the evidence." That reversal sounds like a formality. It is not. Watch it
    earn its keep on a real example.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fraud-screening model flags 99 percent of genuinely fraudulent claims, and wrongly
    flags 5 percent of honest ones. If 1 percent of all claims are fraudulent, what is the
    chance that a flagged claim actually is fraudulent? Most people say "about 99
    percent". Let us count instead, out of 10,000 claims. Of these, 100 are fraudulent and
    99 of them get flagged. The other 9,900 are honest, and 5 percent of them — 495 — get
    flagged too. So among the 594 flags, only 99 are real fraud: 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ 16.7
    percent. Nearly five out of six flags are false alarms — not because the model is bad,
    but because honest claims vastly outnumber fraudulent ones. Actuaries meet this daily,
    in fraud triage, in underwriting evidence, and in every "the test is 99 percent
    accurate" headline.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — the 99 percent trap" widget="spot-error" config={atBayesSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Combinatorics</em> — the art of counting possibilities — is the engine beneath
    every probability, and two patterns cover most of the ground. <strong>Combinations</strong>
    count selections where order does not matter: choosing 3 of 20 claims at random for
    audit gives C(20,3) = 1,140 distinct possible audit sets. And the
    <strong>birthday surprise</strong> shows why naive intuition misleads: in an office of
    23 people, the chance that two share a birthday is above 50 percent — because the
    question is really about 253 pairs of people, not 23. Insurance books are full of such
    pairings: policies sharing one flood zone, one supplier, one storm. The counting
    instinct — "how many ways can this go wrong?" — is what separates sound modelling from
    wishful addition.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Independence</strong> is the property that lets probabilities multiply. Two
    events are independent exactly when learning one does not change the probability of
    the other — in symbols, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Most actuarial modelling is an
    argument about whether that equation applies. Car accidents by separate drivers in
    separate towns: plausibly yes. Fire losses across the flats of one building:
    absolutely not — one fire takes the whole floor. Multiplication builds big models from
    small ones; its misuse builds big mistakes from small assumptions. Every independence
    claim deserves the question a regulator would ask: what happens in the year the
    equation fails?
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the pairs — may the probabilities multiply?" widget="sort-buckets" config={atIndependenceBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> P(A given B) and P(B given A) are interchangeable. They
  are not, and confusing them is the <em>prosecutor's fallacy</em>: "the match is
  one-in-a-million, so the chance this defendant is innocent is one-in-a-million" ignores
  that a million-odd people also match. In the fraud example: P(fraud given flag) ≈ 17
  percent while P(flag given fraud) = 99 percent — the same evidence, two very different
  probabilities, and the whole of Bayesian practice lives in the space between.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — evidence and updates" widget="judge" config={atBayesJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> evidence updates probabilities (Bayes), counting possibilities
    keeps the update honest, and independence decides when probabilities may be
    multiplied. Next: putting the uncertainty itself into a shape you can price.
  </p>

  <h2>Random variables and distributions</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>random variable</em> is simply a quantity whose value is uncertain — the number
    of claims next quarter, the size of the next claim. A <em>distribution</em> is its
    shape: which values occur, with what probabilities. A small catalogue of standard
    shapes covers most situations, and each has a story you already know.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>binomial</strong> counts successes in a fixed number of independent trials
    that each succeed with the same probability — how many of 2,000 policies make a claim.
    Its mean is n·p and its variance is n·p·(1 − p). The <strong>Poisson</strong> counts
    rare events arriving in time or space — claims per week, hurricanes per season — and
    its one parameter sets both its mean and its variance, which is the signature of
    "events at a steady rate, independently". The <strong>exponential</strong> models the
    waiting time between such events, and it is <em>memoryless</em>: having waited ten
    minutes tells you nothing about the next ten. It is the mathematically pure form of
    "the light will not change faster because you have waited". The
    <strong>normal</strong> — the bell curve — is the shape that emerges when many small
    independent effects add up. And the <strong>heavy-tailed</strong> families, such as the
    lognormal and Pareto, model claim <em>severities</em>: sizes of losses, where a fire
    that totals a factory must remain possible.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="fill the blanks — the distribution catalogue" widget="cloze" config={atDistCloze} />
  <p>
    The actuary's first modelling decision follows from that catalogue: total loss equals
    the <em>number</em> of claims times the <em>size</em> of each claim, and the two
    pieces behave differently. Counts are usually Poisson-ish and well behaved. Severities
    carry the heavy tails. Choosing them separately, then combining, is what makes the
    arithmetic of a whole insurance book tractable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three standard lenses are worth naming once. The <em>cumulative distribution
    function</em> F(x) answers "what is the probability of landing at or under x?" — and
    the pricing question "what loss level will one year in twenty exceed?" is exactly the
    reverse lookup: find the 95th percentile. Variables come in two kinds:
    <em>discrete</em> ones take separated values (0, 1, 2 claims) and are described by
    point probabilities; <em>continuous</em> ones take ranges, where the probability of
    one exact value is zero and only intervals carry weight. And a distribution can be
    transformed: an insurer's deductible turns every claim size X into a payment of
    (X − d) once X is large enough — a random variable built from a random variable, which
    the ordinary machinery of expectation and variance handles without ceremony.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> uncertain quantities have distributions, and each situation
    suggests a shape — binomial for counts of trials, Poisson for rare arrivals,
    exponential for waits, normal for sums of small effects, heavy tails for claim sizes.
    Now the two summary numbers that turn shapes into prices.
  </p>

  <h2>Expectation, variance and moments</h2>
  <p>
    The <em>expectation</em> is the long-run average: the probability-weighted centre of
    the distribution, and the only defensible starting point for a price. Its superpower
    is <em>linearity</em> — the expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations, with
    no independence required — which is why an insurer can price a million very different
    policies by simply adding up their expected costs. The <em>variance</em> measures the
    average squared spread around that centre; its square root, the
    <strong>standard deviation</strong>, is the "typical miss" — the size of a bad year.
    Higher <em>moments</em> refine the portrait: skewness asks whether losses lean to the
    right, kurtosis asks how fat the far tail is. They are called moments for the same
    reason masses in mechanics are — each one is a weighted average of a higher power.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now run the village arithmetic at real scale, with the widget's defaults: 2,000
    policies, each with a 5 percent chance of a 20,000-dollar claim. The expected total
    claims are 2,000 × 0.05 × 20,000 = 2,000,000 dollars — so the fair premium is 1,000
    dollars per policy, before expenses and profit. But the total is random, and its
    standard deviation is √(n·p·(1 − p)) × severity = √95 × 20,000 ≈ 195,000 dollars —
    about 10 percent of the expected total. Now quadruple the book to 8,000 policies. The
    expected loss quadruples, but the standard deviation only doubles, because it grows
    with √n — so the relative uncertainty <em>halves</em>. That 1-over-√n shrinkage is the
    law of large numbers wearing a business suit. Scale itself is the oldest
    risk-reduction technology, and pooling — not prediction — is insurance's core
    competence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the magnitude — the pool's typical miss" widget="estimate" config={atSdEstimate} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> independence and "having nothing to do with each
  other" are the same thing. They are not. Independence is a precise condition — learning
  one event does not change the probability of the other — and it can hold or fail in
  surprising places. Two flats' fire losses are related in everyday language and
  statistically dependent; two distant drivers' accidents may be dependent too, if both
  depend on the same icy weather. The test is always the multiplication rule, never the
  vibe.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="expected cost, loading, and volatility of the pool" widget="sliderLab" config={premCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> with the default sliders, the expected claims readout
    sits at 2,000,000 dollars and the premium per policy at 1,000 dollars before the load.
    Now quadruple the number of policies: the expected claims readout quadruples with it,
    but the standard deviation only doubles — it grows with the square root of the pool
    size, so the uncertainty relative to the expected total falls. Finally slide the profit
    loading and watch it sit on top of the fair price rather than change what the claims
    are expected to cost.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <em>central limit theorem</em> then ties everything together: sums of many
    independent contributions — like a large pool of mostly claim-free policies — come out
    approximately normal, whatever the individual shapes were. That is why the normal
    distribution haunts aggregate pricing, and why "within two standard deviations" is
    meaningful shorthand even when nobody's individual loss is remotely bell-shaped.
  </p>
  <p>
    Skewness earns a final, practical note. In heavy-tailed loss distributions, the
    <em>mean sits above the median</em> — often by a lot. The typical claim is small:
    most fender-benders, most power-outages. But the premium must be set by the mean,
    because the rare enormous claims really arrive and really must be paid. This is why
    "average claim" in a press release and "expected loss" in a rate filing are different
    numbers with different jobs — and why reinsurance exists: the tail above some
    threshold is worth selling to someone whose balance sheet is sized for it. Price the
    mean, capitalise the tail, and never confuse the two. That is the whole profession in
    one sentence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet first — the press release vs the rate filing" widget="predict" config={atMeanPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the expected value is the outcome you expect. It is
  not — it is the long-run average, and it may not be possible on any single run: the
  expected number of children per family might be 2.4, yet no family has 2.4 children. An
  insurer expects 2,000,000 dollars of claims and knows the actual figure will miss that
  mark; the standard deviation exists precisely to say by how much.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why can the village treasurer forecast the year's fires when no family can forecast its own?</li>
    <li>A screening tool catches 99 percent of frauds and falsely flags 5 percent of honest claims, and 1 percent of claims are fraudulent. Roughly what share of its flags are real fraud?</li>
    <li>The insurance pool doubles in size. What happens to the expected total loss, and to the standard deviation relative to that expected loss?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Individual events are unpredictable, but large groups of
    similar events are predictable — with 100 houses the total number of fires settles
    down near its long-run average, which is what the fund is sized against. (2) About 17
    percent: count 10,000 claims and 99 real frauds are flagged alongside 495 honest
    claims, so 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ 16.7 percent — the small base rate dominates. (3) The
    expected total doubles, but the standard deviation grows only as √n, so relative to
    the expected loss it falls — by a factor of √2 — which is why bigger pools are safer.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One policy is unpredictable; a large pool of similar policies is remarkably predictable.</li>
    <li>Conditional probability and Bayes' theorem turn new evidence into an updated chance — and the base rate decides how much to trust a positive flag.</li>
    <li>Total loss combines two random pieces: how many claims (frequency) and how large each one is (severity).</li>
    <li>Expectation sets the price floor; the standard deviation sizes the capital that absorbs a bad year.</li>
    <li>Probabilities may be multiplied only under independence — the test is the multiplication rule, never the vibe.</li>
    <li>Quadrupling the pool quadruples the expected loss but only doubles the standard deviation, so relative uncertainty falls.</li>
    <li>Price the mean, capitalise the tail — and never confuse the two.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Later lessons push the same two questions further. Ruin theory asks directly, with
    premium income and random claims, what the probability is that the fund ever falls
    below zero; credibility theory blends a group's own experience with the class average;
    and interest theory adds time, pricing money across dates in pensions and life
    products.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The toolkit has deeper rooms. <em>Moment-generating functions</em> encode all the
    moments of a distribution in one object, and their payoffs are practical: sums of
    independent variables get MGFs that multiply, which makes aggregate loss distributions
    computable — and an MGF, when it exists, identifies the distribution completely, the
    rigorous version of "this looks normal". <em>Joint distributions and covariance</em>
    quantify how variables move together, and they sharpen the limits of pooling: the
    1-over-√n smoothness assumes independence, and that assumption fails spectacularly
    when policies share a peril — hurricanes, wildfires and cyber events hit thousands of
    policies at once, converting "diversified" books into single bets. This is why
    catastrophe insurance is reinsurance-heavy: correlation, not frequency, is what
    actually breaks insurers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later theory builds upward from the same base. <em>Ruin theory</em> asks the founding
    question directly — with premium income and random claims, what is the probability
    the fund ever falls below zero? — and answers it with the adjustment coefficient and
    Cramér–Lundberg bounds, turning "hold enough capital" into a formula.
    <em>Credibility theory</em> blends a group's own experience with the class average,
    weighting each by how much trust the data deserves — Bayes made actuarial. The
    profession's later exams add time: interest theory prices money across dates
    (discounting, annuities, bonds), and life contingencies fuses mortality tables with
    discount factors into the valuation of pensions and life products. The open frontier
    is statistical: heavier-than-Pareto tails in a warming climate, correlated systemic
    risks, and machine-learned pricing that regulators now police for bias — the same two
    questions, price and capital, in an era with sharper tools and sharper edges.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SOA Exam P/FM syllabi; Ross, <em>A First Course in Probability</em>; Klugman, Panjer &amp; Willmot, <em>Loss Models</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
